{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 18,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the coaching employment, the separate oral promise to buy out Haywood’s Miami contract, and the University’s reliance on just-cause termination to defeat his payment claims at summary judgment."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Understanding “all further obligations” requires ordinary contract interpretation, not knowledge of college athletics regulation or Indiana’s criminal diversion process."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must compare paragraph 14.1 with the use of “hereunder” in paragraphs 14.4 and 14.10 and understand its asserted effect on the separate oral buyout agreement."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Lines 265–269 expressly state the competing scopes of “all further obligations” and explain why the court adopted the broader reading."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "d01 and d02 concern the same words and outcome, so the reviewer must distinguish the scope question from the cross-provision comparison supporting its resolution."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d02: It requires comparing paragraph 14.1’s unrestricted wording with “hereunder” in paragraphs 14.4 and 14.10, then distinguishing that whole-contract comparison from d01’s scope question. The court makes the connection explicit, so the additional difficulty is modest.",
  "reason": "This case is suitable for a general contract reviewer with a short contextual read. The central dispute is readily stated: whether just-cause termination extinguished only employment-contract obligations or also the separate Miami buyout obligation. Read the oral-agreement discussion far enough to understand why that promise remained relevant despite the integration clause, then focus on lines 260–270 and the compared termination provisions. The reviewer need not reconstruct the detailed arrest and termination chronology or study the diversion stipulation to understand the listed defects. The main review risk is conflating d01’s scope issue with d02’s textual comparison, not uncertainty about what the court construed."
}
